<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kubadabad Palace</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kubadabad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kubadabad Palace was a summer palace of the Seljuk sultans of Rum on the south-western shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-beysehir">Lake Beyşehir</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, built for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayqubad-i">Kayqubad I</a> in the 1230s. Excavations have uncovered its halls and many glazed tiles with figures of people, animals and mythical creatures, now mostly in the Karatay Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>, a rare view of Seljuk court art.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kubadabad-palace.jpg" alt="Kubadabad Palace" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saadet Armağan Güleç Korumaz, Ferruh Yıldız · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kubadabad_general_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
